    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Junior Caminero** serving as our primary offensive engine, boasting a standout 77.12 score. His matchup against Trevor Rogers is heavily amplified by a +16% park factor, creating an ideal environment for him to exploit the favorable hitting conditions despite the minor platoon friction. Joining him in the top tier are **Yordan Alvarez** (75.72) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (68.54). **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in elite asset given his massive 0.460 xwOBA, while **Pete Crow-Armstrong** provides critical value as our primary speed threat; his dynamic platoon advantage against Emerson Hancock makes him a high-efficiency start despite a slight park penalty. Meanwhile, **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (65.08) sneaks into the utility slot as an aggressive play, benefiting significantly from a high-run-environment park and the slow delivery of Freddy Peralta, which should gift him extra opportunities to secure an opportunistic stolen base.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Chase Meidroth**, who finished just outside the starting nine with a 44.05 score. While **Chase Meidroth** offered decent utility, he was ultimately edged out by **Lars Nootbaar** and the broader depth of the roster; his score was suppressed by a poor lineup projection and a 37% rain risk in his matchup against Sean Manaea. Risk mitigation remains a focal point of today\u2019s strategy, particularly with **Carson Benge** (47.96) carrying that same 37% weather threat against Sean Burke. We are also monitoring **Kazuma Okamoto** (42.61) closely, as he faces a formidable challenge in Cam Schlittler; his score reflects a significant hit from the opposing SP\u2019s 2.84 xERA. As we await final confirmations for **Dillon Dingler**, **Colt Keith**, **Trea Turner**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Lars Nootbaar**, and **Kyle Schwarber**, we remain prepared to adjust should any late lineup shifts impact our projected speed or RBI density.",
